\section{Why this exists rather than car}{
:Anova:
\code{car::Anova()} reads the \code{assign} attribute of the model matrix, which has one
entry per design column. A multinomial fit has \code{C} coefficients per column, so
\code{car} would match a short vector against a long one and silently test only the
first category, reporting \code{df = 1} where the correct joint test has \code{df = C}.
It returns a plausible table that answers the wrong question. \code{illume}
registers its own method so that \code{car::Anova()} dispatches here and gets the
right answer.
